{
  "teamId": 135,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 San Diego Padres burst out of the gate with one of the most dominant Aprils in recent franchise memory, going 18-7 and establishing themselves as one of the National League's premier teams. Jackson Merrill's walk-off double, Manny Machado's three-run blast, and a series of late-game heroics from an unlikely cast of contributors set the tone early — this was a team that could beat you in multiple ways, with depth from top to bottom.\n\nThe summer months tested that depth severely. May and June brought a combined 24-30 record as the rotation showed cracks, relievers like Randy Vásquez and Adrian Morejon surrendered critical home runs, and the offense sputtered through key stretches. But the Padres refused to fold, and pivotal contributions from Luis Campusano's cannon arm behind the plate and a rejuvenated Ty France helped stabilize things through the dog days of July. The team played .500 ball when it mattered most — not pretty, but survivable.\n\nAugust and September were a revelation. A 35-17 two-month stretch erased any doubt about San Diego's postseason credentials, propelled by Jackson Merrill's continued evolution into a star and a Luis Campusano home run in early September that swung a crucial win in the NL West race. The Padres finished 91-71, punched their playoff ticket, and dispatched their Wild Card opponent to set up a date with Milwaukee in the NLDS.\n\nThe Division Series has been a battle from the opening pitch. The Padres scratched across early runs with Campusano's clutch single in Game 1, but Jackson Chourio and the Brewers bullpen have proven formidable, responding with home runs of their own. A painful ninth-inning single off Mason Miller's hand in Game 2 turned a tense contest and shifted momentum Milwaukee's way. Jake Cronenworth's solo shot in Game 3 gave San Diego a spark, and Michael King's double-play ball to escape a bases-loaded jam has kept the series alive. The Padres' October fate is still being written.",
